{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness in this context refers to the distribution of outcomes: a lottery is right-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable (negative) outcomes compared to favorable (positive) ones. In both lotteries, the only possible outcomes are -6000 or -3000 euros (losses), and 0 euros (no loss). Since there are no positive outcomes, the 'favorable' outcomes are only 0 euros, and the 'unfavorable' outcomes are the negative amounts. The probability mass is entirely on the loss outcomes. Thus, the skewness depends on how the probability is distributed across the loss amounts. Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of losing 6000 euros and 99.9% of losing nothing. Lottery B has a 0.2% chance of losing 3000 euros and 99.8% of losing nothing. Since both lotteries have a high probability of no loss (99.9% and 99.8%, respectively), the 'unfavorable' outcomes are rare. However, the distribution of the loss magnitude matters for skewness. A right-skewed lottery would have a larger probability mass on the left (loss side), especially on the larger loss. In this case, Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of a larger loss (-6000) and 99.9% of no loss, while Lottery B has a 0.2% chance of a smaller loss (-3000) and 99.8% of no loss. Thus, Lottery A has a more concentrated probability on a larger loss, which makes it more right-skewed (since a greater portion of probability is on the unfavorable side, specifically the larger loss). Therefore, the decision reason would prefer Lottery A because it is more right-skewed. The relevant information for the decision reason is thus: Lottery A is more right-skewed due to a higher probability of a larger loss (though the probability is small), and thus the reason would prefer A.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'Der Unterschied in der Wahrscheinlichkeit eines Verlusts ist so gering, dass ich mich eher für den in absoluten Zahlen geringeren Verlust entscheiden würde.' This translates to: 'The difference in the probability of a loss is so small that I would rather choose the one with the smaller absolute loss.' The individual focuses on the magnitude of the loss (3000 euros vs. 6000 euros) and concludes that, because the difference in loss probability is negligible, they prefer the lottery with the smaller loss (Lottery B). The individual does not mention skewness, probability distribution, or the shape of the outcome distribution. Instead, the justification is based solely on the absolute size of the loss and the perceived small difference in probability of loss. The deliberation process centers on minimizing the loss amount, not on assessing the skewness of the outcome distribution. Thus, the individual considers the magnitude of potential losses and the small difference in probability, but does not reference the distribution of probabilities across outcomes or the concept of skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires assessing skewness — specifically, whether a lottery has a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes (losses) — and preferring the more right-skewed one. In this case, Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of a -6000 euro loss, while Lottery B has a 0.2% chance of a -3000 euro loss. Although the probability of loss is higher in B, the loss is smaller. Skewness depends on the distribution: a lottery with a larger loss and a small probability (like A) is more right-skewed because the probability mass is concentrated on a larger negative outcome. Thus, A is more right-skewed. The decision reason would prefer A. However, the individual's report does not mention skewness or the shape of the distribution. Instead, the individual justifies the choice based on the smaller absolute loss (3000 vs. 6000), ignoring the probability structure and focusing only on the loss amount. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The information considered (loss magnitude) differs from the required information (distribution of probability across outcomes), and the justification (preference for smaller loss) does not align with the rule of preferring greater right-skewness. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

